On Mon, 12 Jul 2004 09:28:34 UTC, psmithnews@spod-central.org (Dylan 
O'Donnell) wrote:

> die@spammer.die (Mark Healey) writes:
> > I've noticed that monsters deep in the dungeons have more and better
> > stuff than those up high.  That is to be expected, tougher monsters.
> > What I'm wondering is is the inventory generated a function of dungeon
> > depth ormonster toughness.
>
> Executive summary: the latter, generally speaking.
>
> Starting equipment for intelligent monsters comes in two flavours:
>
> Individual monster types may be issued individually-predetermined
> (possibly randomly picked from a few choices, one of which might be
> "none") weapons and/or armour (occasionally other stuff in specific
> cases, such as Orcus' wand of death); the beefier monsters tend on the
> whole to be assigned the better gear, particularly relatively compared
> within a given monster class.
>
> In addition to this, there are randomly-doled-out offensive (attack
> wands, harmful potions, etc), defensive (potions of healing, scrolls
> of teleportation, etc), and miscellaneous (wands of speed monster,
> potions of gain level, etc) items; eligibiity to be assigned these is
> determined by a die roll based against the monster's level, and the
> higher the monster's difficulty, the more effective an item is likely
> to chosen. (The exception is that only _weak_ monsters will be given
> wands or potions of polymorph, on the grounds that they're the ones
> that would need the improvement.)

